--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0004-private-wayland-clipboard-queue.md
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# ADR-0004: Dispatch clipboard transfers on a private Wayland queue
+
+Status: Implemented in the ak5 fork; native protocol validation in progress.
+
+Clipboard ownership and transfers use the window's existing Wayland connection
+and seat. A dedicated worker dispatches only clipboard proxies on a private
+`wl_event_queue`. The window and Vulkan WSI retain their own queues. Every reader
+uses Wayland's prepare/read/cancel protocol. No window, rendering, input-model or
+JSI object moves onto the clipboard worker.
+
+Seat callbacks publish keyboard focus and valid press serials atomically. Writes
+require both, advertise a fresh source, and complete only when the compositor
+returns that source's correlation MIME type as the selection. Reads following a
+write wait for its acknowledgement. The correlation type identifies a request;
+it does not grant authority. The compositor and seat serial supply authority.
+
+Transfer pipes are nonblocking. Reads accept complete, valid UTF-8 up to 64 MiB;
+timeouts, malformed text and oversized transfers fail without applying data.
+Pending requests and transfers have bounded queues. SIGPIPE is blocked only in
+the worker, so a disappearing receiver cannot terminate the application.
+
+Backend callbacks return promptly. JavaScript promises settle through CallInvoker
+on the JS thread. Text input polls futures while frames continue; a delayed cut
+or paste applies only if the original field family, text, event count, selection
+and edit permissions still match. Focus changes and unmounts cancel pending edits.
+The window stops and joins the worker before releasing its seat or display.
+Missing compositor capabilities report unavailable clipboard access, rather than
+using the headless process-local store in a GUI application.
+
+Verification includes native controller delayed-result tests, concurrent backend
+registry tests, strict UTF-8 cases, real compositor copy/paste with a second client,
+large payloads, stalled owners, malformed data and shutdown with pending work.
+ASan/UBSan and TSan must exercise the transport as well as the registry.
